  callers="CALLERS: `runIssuerCertSweep()` is run once at service start and every 6 h from `startIssuerCertSweep` (called from `src/index.ts`); it also runs cross-tenant under the platform scope. WHAT AN ORDINARY ERROR GETS (the rule for taking the sites): at line 66 an ordinary rejection of `markExpiredCerts` is logged and the sweep CONTINUES to the three expiring-soon windows (the file's own comment says so); at line 95 an ordinary rejection of `findExpiringSoon(days)` is logged and the loop `continue`s to the next window; at line 83 an ordinary rejection of the ISSUER_CERT_EXPIRED publish is logged as a warning and nothing else happens. Today (hostile value): at 66 the catch throws, so `runIssuerCertSweepInner` rejects BEFORE the expiring-soon pass: no ISSUER_CERT_EXPIRING_SOON events are published and the caller (the timer's `.catch`) receives a rejection; at 95 the catch throws and the REMAINING windows are skipped the same way; at 83 the `.catch` arrow throws, so the promise it returns rejects with nothing attached: an unhandled rejection. After: each site behaves exactly as for an ordinary Error. SWEEP CONTINUATION: the fix DOES change whether the sweep continues past a failure, and only toward the ordinary-Error behaviour: after a hostile failure of `markExpiredCerts` or `findExpiringSoon(days)` the sweep now goes on to the remaining windows and publishes their ISSUER_CERT_EXPIRING_SOON events, exactly as it does for an ordinary Error today. It writes or deletes NOTHING that an ordinary Error path would not: the failing call is the only database operation of its step (the expire UPDATE already failed and is not retried; `findExpiringSoon` is a read), the events published by the continuation are the same events an ordinary-Error path publishes, and the expired rows of a failed `markExpiredCerts` are not known so none is published. **BEHAVIOUR CHANGE the raise PR must state:** for an unreadable or non-string-message thrown value only, the sweep no longer aborts at a failed `markExpiredCerts` / `findExpiringSoon` (the remaining expiring-soon windows run and publish their events) and a failed event publish is logged as a warning instead of becoming an unhandled rejection; a Symbol message is logged as its text; every string message and every non-Error throw behave exactly as before; edge: an Error whose own `message` is `undefined` was logged with no `error` value and now logs the text 'undefined'.",
